        <div id="lumens-runtime" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Lumens vs Runtime: The Trade-Off on Every Box
          </h2>

          <p>
            Lantern packaging leads with lumens because big numbers sell. A 1,500-lumen lantern sounds three times better than a 500-lumen one. In practice, the top setting is the one you will use least, and it drains the battery fastest. The number that matters in a power outage is how many hours of useful light you get at a brightness you will actually use.
          </p>

          <p>
            Some practical reference points. Around 50 to 100 lumens lights a hallway, a bathroom or a bedside table well enough to move around safely. About 200 to 300 lumens lights a dinner table or a small room for reading and playing cards. Around 500 to 600 lumens lights a kitchen for cooking. More than that is mostly useful outdoors or in a garage. Most households in an outage live on the 100 to 300 lumen settings.
          </p>

          <p>
            Runtime falls off steeply as brightness rises. The Coleman Classic Recharge is a good example: Coleman rates it at about 5 hours at 800 lumens, 15 hours at 300 and 45 hours at 100. Cutting the brightness by a little over half triples the runtime, and cutting it to an eighth multiplies runtime by nine. The same pattern holds for every lantern on this list.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Use</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Useful brightness</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">What to look for</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Hallway or bathroom nightlight</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">20-100 lumens</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">A low mode rated for 100+ hours</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Bedroom or reading</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">100-250 lumens</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Warm light and a dimmer</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Dinner table or living room</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">200-400 lumens</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">A medium mode rated for 10+ hours</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Kitchen and cooking</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">400-600 lumens</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Wide, glare-free diffusion</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Garage, yard or campsite</td>
