                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">A high mode, ideally with a backup power source</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Also treat headline lumen numbers with some skepticism. Owners of several big-number lanterns report that the light looks dimmer than the box suggests, and test standards vary. A well-diffused 500-lumen lantern with warm light often feels brighter and more useful in a room than a harsh 1,000-lumen lantern that throws glare in your eyes.
          </p>

          <p>
            The best way to compare lanterns is to look for the rated hours at the 200 to 300 lumen range. A lantern that gives you 15 hours there will carry a household through two or three evenings on one charge, which is what an outage lantern needs to do.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="power-source" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Rechargeable vs AA and D Cells
          </h2>

          <p>
            This is the most important choice you will make, and it is not obvious. Rechargeable lanterns are cheaper to run, better for the environment and often include USB output. Disposable-cell lanterns are more reliable after long storage. The best outage setup uses both.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Rechargeable lanterns</h3>

          <p>
            A rechargeable lantern with a built-in lithium-ion battery costs essentially nothing per hour to run and can be topped up from a wall outlet, a power bank, a car or a solar panel. The problem is self-discharge and forgetfulness. A lithium-ion lantern loses some charge sitting on a shelf, and if nobody charged it after the last camping trip, it may be nearly flat when a storm knocks the power out. The fix is a routine: charge every lantern when you change the clocks in spring and fall.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">AA and D-cell lanterns</h3>

          <p>
            Alkaline cells keep most of their charge for years, which makes a D-cell lantern like the Streamlight Siege the most dependable light in a closet. D cells also hold a lot of energy, which is why D-cell lanterns run for hundreds of hours on low. The downsides are weight, cost per hour and the risk of leaking cells. Store spare cells outside the lantern, and check them once a year.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Hybrid lanterns</h3>

          <p>
            The best of both is a lantern that runs on a rechargeable battery and accepts disposable cells as a backup, like the Duracell 1,500-lumen hybrid and the Black Diamond Apollo. Use the rechargeable battery day to day, and when an outage starts with a flat lantern, drop in cells. If you only buy one lantern, a hybrid is the safest choice.
          </p>

          <p>
            A note on rechargeable AA and D cells: low-self-discharge NiMH cells, such as Panasonic eneloop, hold their charge well in storage and work in most disposable-cell lanterns. They are a good middle ground if you already own a charger, though they produce slightly lower voltage than alkalines and some lanterns run a little dimmer on them.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="usb-out" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. USB Power-Out Lanterns: Useful, Within Limits
          </h2>

          <p>
            Many rechargeable lanterns now include a USB output, marketed as a power bank feature. It is genuinely useful in an outage, but it helps to know how little energy a lantern actually holds compared with a dedicated battery.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Rated Battery Capacity</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">9.6Wh</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Black Diamond Apollo (2,600mAh)</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">18.7Wh</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Goal Zero Lighthouse 600</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">23.6Wh</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">BioLite AlpenGlow 500</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">~88Wh</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">A 24,000mAh power bank</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            A typical current phone battery holds around 13 to 19Wh, and charging loses roughly a third of the energy along the way. That means a lantern with an 18.7Wh battery can give a phone about one partial to one full charge, and then it has nothing left for light. The BioLite's larger battery gets you roughly one full phone charge. A small lantern like the Apollo manages an emergency top-up.
          </p>

          <p>
            So treat the USB port as a lifeline, not a plan. It is valuable when a phone is about to die and you need to make a call, check a weather alert or reach family. For keeping several phones charged through a multi-day outage, a dedicated power bank does the job far better; our guide to the <a href="/best-portable-charger-for-power-outages/">best portable chargers for power outages</a> compares them, and a 24,000mAh bank holds four or five times the energy of most lanterns.
          </p>

          <p>
            The smarter direction is the reverse: use a power bank or power station to recharge the lantern. A lantern drawing a few watts on medium can be refilled many times from a mid-size power station, which is why a rechargeable lantern pairs so well with a battery backup.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="outage-vs-camping" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Camping Lantern vs Power Outage Lantern
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most lanterns are sold as camping gear, and most work fine in a blackout. But the priorities differ, and a lantern that is perfect for one can be mediocre for the other.
          </p>

          <p>
            For camping, weight, size, water resistance and ruggedness matter most. You want a lantern that packs small, survives a drop on rocks, shrugs off rain and runs a weekend on one charge. Brightness matters less, because a tent or a picnic table is a small space, and a warm, dim light is more pleasant than a bright one under the stars. The Fenix CL30R, the BioLite AlpenGlow and the Black Diamond Apollo are all built with the campsite in mind.
          </p>

          <p>
            For a power outage, runtime, reliable storage and the ability to light a whole room count more. A lantern for a power outage sits unused for months, then has to run for several evenings in a row, possibly without any way to recharge. That favors a big battery, a D-cell backup, a crank or solar input, and a warm light bright enough for a kitchen. Weight does not matter at all on a kitchen table. The Goal Zero Lighthouse 600, the Streamlight Siege and the Duracell hybrid are strongest here.
          </p>

          <p>
            Light quality matters in both. Cheap lanterns use cold, blue-white LEDs that make a room feel harsh. Look for a warm color temperature, around 2,700K to 3,500K, or a lantern with a warm mode. A frosted or diffused globe spreads light evenly and avoids the glare of a bare LED, which is especially important when a lantern sits at eye level on a table.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you want one lantern for both uses, buy a rechargeable model with a large battery and USB input, then keep a cheap D-cell lantern as a backstop for outages. That covers a camping weekend and a three-day blackout.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="candles-gas" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Why Not Candles or Gas Lanterns Indoors
          </h2>

          <p>
            Candles are the traditional outage light, and they are one of the most common causes of house fires during outages. People fall asleep with them lit, kids and pets knock them over, and a candle near a curtain or a stack of papers can start a fire in seconds. The US Fire Administration and the American Red Cross both advise using flashlights and battery powered lanterns instead of candles during a power outage. A battery lantern is cheaper than a single home insurance deductible and does the job better.
          </p>

          <p>
            Fuel-burning lanterns are a bigger problem. Propane, butane, white gas and kerosene lanterns are designed for outdoor use. Indoors, they consume oxygen, produce carbon monoxide and burn with an open flame or a hot mantle, and their manufacturers warn against using them inside homes, tents or other enclosed spaces. Carbon monoxide is colorless and odorless, and poisoning cases spike during outages because people run fuel-burning equipment indoors.
          </p>

          <p>
            The same logic applies to generators. Never run a portable generator in a house, a garage or anywhere near open windows, and make sure you have working battery-powered carbon monoxide alarms. Our guide to the <a href="/best-home-generator-for-power-outages/">best home generators for power outages</a> covers safe placement. Battery power stations avoid the exhaust problem entirely, and our guide to whether <a href="/are-portable-power-stations-safe-indoors/">portable power stations are safe indoors</a> explains the precautions that still apply.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">The Rule</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Inside the house, light comes from batteries. Flames and fuel stay outside.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">A battery powered lantern cannot start a fire if it tips over and produces no carbon monoxide. Keep one per main room, plus a flashlight per person, and leave candles and gas lanterns for the patio.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            If you want the cozy feel of candlelight during a long outage, a lantern with a warm flicker mode, like the BioLite AlpenGlow, comes surprisingly close without the risk.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="small-lights" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Small Battery Operated Lights and Lamps Around the House
          </h2>

          <p>
            A single bright lantern on the kitchen table does not light a whole house. The households that cope best with outages spread several small battery operated lights around the home, so nobody walks down a dark staircase or fumbles in a pitch-black bathroom.
          </p>

          <p>
            Start with the paths people walk at night: stairs, hallways and the route to the bathroom. Small battery operated lights with motion sensors are ideal here. Stick-on LED puck lights and motion-activated light bars run on AA or AAA cells or a small rechargeable battery, and they turn on only when someone walks past, so the batteries last for weeks. Install them before an outage, not during one.
          </p>

          <p>
            Bedrooms and bathrooms need a small battery operated lamp with a low mode. The Black Diamond Apollo is the best example on our list; it sits on a nightstand like a lamp and runs all night on its lowest setting. For a child's room, a lantern with a dim warm mode is reassuring and safe to leave on.
          </p>

          <p>
            Everyone should also have a flashlight or headlamp of their own. A lantern lights a room, but a headlamp keeps both hands free for cooking, checking the breaker panel or carrying a child. Keep them in a known spot, such as a kitchen drawer or the nightstand, so you can find them in the dark.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, do not overlook the light you already own. Rechargeable work lights from your cordless tool system run for hours on a tool battery, and a phone flashlight is fine for a minute or two. Do not rely on the phone, though; its battery is too valuable for communication to spend on light.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="household-plan" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. How Many Lanterns a Household Needs
          </h2>

          <p>
            A practical rule: one main lantern for each room where people gather, one small light for each bedroom and bathroom, and one flashlight or headlamp per person. For a typical family of four, that works out to two main lanterns, three or four small lights and four headlamps. That sounds like a lot, but a well-chosen set costs less than a single night in a hotel.
          </p>

          <p>
            Mix power sources. Make the main lanterns rechargeable for cheap, everyday use, and make at least one of them a hybrid or D-cell model so you have reliable light even if every rechargeable is flat. Keep spare AA and D cells in a sealed bag in the same place as the lanterns.
          </p>

          <p>
            Plan for recharging. A rechargeable lantern is only as good as your ability to refill it. A large power bank refills a mid-size lantern several times, and a portable power station refills every light in the house many times over; see our <a href="/best-portable-power-station-for-camping/">camping power station picks</a> for compact options that work in both settings. For long outages, a folding solar panel keeps the cycle going, and our <a href="/best-portable-solar-panel/">portable solar panel guide</a> covers the ones that pair with small power stations.
          </p>

          <p>
            Think about how long your outages usually last. If they are a few hours, two lanterns and flashlights are plenty. If you live somewhere hurricanes, ice storms or wildfire shutoffs cause multi-day outages, prioritize runtime and recharging, and consider a power station that can also run a fridge and a fan. Our <a href="/best-portable-charger-for-power-outages/">power outage charger guide</a> is the place to start building that kit.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="storage" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Storing Lanterns and Batteries So They Work When Needed
          </h2>

          <p>
            The most common lantern failure is not a defect. It is a lantern stored with a flat battery, or with alkaline cells that leaked and corroded the contacts. A few habits prevent both.
          </p>

          <p>
            Charge rechargeable lanterns every three to six months, and after every use. Pick a memorable trigger, such as the daylight saving time changes or the start of hurricane season, and charge every lantern and power bank in the house on the same day. Lithium-ion batteries prefer to be stored partly charged in a cool place, but for an emergency light, keeping it full matters more.
          </p>

          <p>
            Store alkaline cells outside the lantern. Leaking alkaline batteries are the leading cause of dead D-cell lanterns, and the leak happens slowly while the lantern sits in a closet. Keep the cells in their package or a sealed bag next to the lantern, and put them in when you need light. Check expiration dates once a year.
          </p>

          <p>
            Keep everything in one place everyone knows. A shelf in a hall closet or a labeled bin near the breaker panel works. Include the lanterns, spare cells, headlamps, the USB cables each lantern needs, and a power bank. In an outage, nobody should have to search a dark house for a charging cable.
          </p>

          <p>
            Test the whole kit once a year. Turn each lantern on, run it for a few minutes on every setting, and replace anything that flickers or fails. Ten minutes of testing on a sunny afternoon is much better than discovering a dead lantern during a storm.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying by the biggest lumen number</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A 1,500-lumen top setting sounds great but drains the battery in a few hours, and you will rarely use it indoors. Compare rated runtime at 200 to 300 lumens instead, and favor warm, well-diffused light. A soft 500-lumen lantern usually lights a room better than a glaring 1,000-lumen one.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Storing a rechargeable lantern flat</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Lithium-ion lanterns lose charge on the shelf, and the one nobody charged after the last camping trip is the one that is dead when the storm hits. Charge every lantern after use and at least twice a year, and keep at least one disposable-cell or hybrid lantern as a backstop.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Leaving alkaline cells inside the lantern</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Alkaline batteries slowly leak in storage, and the corrosion can ruin the contacts of an otherwise good D-cell lantern. Store spare cells in a sealed bag next to the lantern instead of inside it, check their dates once a year, and load them only when you actually need light.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using gas lanterns or candles indoors</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Propane, butane and kerosene lanterns release carbon monoxide and are made for outdoor use only, and candles are a leading cause of outage house fires. Inside the house, light should come from batteries. Keep fuel lanterns on the patio and make sure your carbon monoxide alarms have working batteries.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Relying on the lantern to charge phones</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A lantern's USB port is handy, but most lantern batteries hold only 10 to 25Wh, roughly one phone charge. Spend that and you lose your light. Keep a dedicated power bank for phones, and use it or a power station to recharge the lantern rather than the other way around.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <PlanTheJob />

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best battery powered lantern for a power outage?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The Goal Zero Lighthouse 600 is the best all-round pick. It gives up to 600 lumens of warm light, recharges from USB, solar or a hand crank, and can top up a phone. Pair it with a D-cell lantern such as the Streamlight Siege, which stays ready in storage for years, so you have reliable light even if the rechargeable is flat.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a rechargeable lantern better than one that uses D batteries?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For everyday use, yes; a rechargeable lantern costs almost nothing to run and can be refilled from a power bank or solar panel. For long storage, D cells win because they hold their charge for years. The best answer is a hybrid lantern that takes both, or one rechargeable lantern plus one D-cell lantern as a backstop.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How many lumens do I need in a lantern for a power outage?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Around 200 to 300 lumens lights a dinner table or living room, and 400 to 600 lumens is enough to cook by. Bedrooms and hallways need only 50 to 100 lumens. Buy a lantern that reaches 500 lumens or more, then run it on medium most of the time, because runtime drops sharply on high.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is the Duracell lantern any good?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The Duracell 1,500-lumen hybrid lantern is a good value. It runs on a built-in rechargeable battery with a four D-cell backup, has a USB output for a phone, and is splash resistant. Duracell sells several lantern versions with different specs, so check the model number and the rated runtimes on the box before buying.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I use a camping lantern during a power outage?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, as long as it is battery powered. LED camping lanterns work well indoors. Never use propane, butane, white gas or kerosene camping lanterns inside a home; they produce carbon monoxide and use an open flame. For outages, favor a lantern with a large battery or disposable-cell backup and a warm light that fills a room.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a lantern charge my phone?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Many rechargeable lanterns have a USB output, but their batteries are small. A lantern with an 18.7Wh battery gives roughly one phone charge and then has nothing left for light. Treat the port as an emergency backup and use a dedicated power bank to keep phones charged through a multi-day outage. Better still, use the power bank to recharge the lantern.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why shouldn't I use candles during a power outage?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Candles cause many house fires during outages, usually when they are left unattended, knocked over or placed near something flammable. Fire safety agencies recommend battery powered lights instead. An LED lantern cannot start a fire if it tips over, gives more light than several candles, and runs for days on one charge or one set of batteries.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
