          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            For most households, the Goal Zero Lighthouse 600 is the battery powered lantern to buy first: warm, bright enough for a kitchen, rechargeable from USB, solar or a crank, and able to rescue a dying phone. Back it up with a Streamlight Siege and a spare set of D cells for the outage that starts with a flat lantern, and add a Black Diamond Apollo or two for bedrooms. On a tighter budget, two Coleman Classic Recharge lanterns and the Duracell hybrid cover a whole house for very little.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Then do the unglamorous part: charge every lantern twice a year, keep spare cells outside the lanterns, and store everything in one place the whole family knows. Leave candles and gas lanterns outside, and keep a power bank for phones so the lantern can stay a lantern. That combination turns a multi-day blackout from a scramble in the dark into a manageable inconvenience.
